const BRANCHES = [
  // by subject
  { id: "metaphysics", n: "Metaphysics", k: "branch", d: "The study of what exists and the most general structure of reality: being, dependence, identity, possibility, time and causation.", q: ["What exists, and what is fundamental?", "What makes distinct things alike?", "How can something change yet remain the same thing?", "What could have been otherwise?", "Are time and causation fundamental features of reality?"], p: ["parmenides", "aristotle", "ibnsina", "spinoza", "leibniz", "hegel", "heidegger"], st: ["Aristotle, Metaphysics", "Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641)", "Spinoza, Ethics (1677)", "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781)", "Michael J. Loux, Metaphysics: A Contemporary Introduction (3rd ed., 2006)", "Alyssa Ney, Metaphysics: An Introduction (2014)"], rel: ["epistemology", "mind", "religion", "logic"], x: "Aristotle called this inquiry first philosophy: the investigation of being insofar as it is being and of first principles and causes. These descriptions are not trivially equivalent, since investigating everything as a being seems different from identifying its ultimate explanation. Later editors gave his treatises the name Metaphysics. Early modern philosophers broadened the field to include mind and body, freedom and the soul; Christian Wolff distinguished general metaphysics (ontology) from special metaphysics. Today metaphysics asks both what exists and how reality is organized.\n\nOntology asks what kinds of entities our best theories commit us to: material things, numbers, properties, events, possible worlds or institutions. Quine proposed examining what a theory must quantify over, although rival formulations and paraphrases can produce different commitments. Contemporary metaphysicians also distinguish existence from fundamentality: a table might exist while depending on its parts. Grounding and ontological dependence address what explains or constitutes what, and whether an explanatory hierarchy has a fundamental level.\n\nThe problem of universals begins with a deceptively ordinary observation: two different apples can both be red. Do they share literally the same property, or are they merely similar particulars? Realists accept universals of some sort; nominalists seek explanations without them. Some realists regard universals as independent of their instances, others as immanent in things. Trope theories instead posit particularized properties, such as this apple's redness. The debate matters for scientific classification, predication and laws of nature, not just color words.\n\nQuestions of substance and composition ask what individual things are. Is a thing a bearer of properties, a bundle of them or a unity of matter and form? Mereology investigates parts and wholes. A statue and its clay seem to occupy the same region, yet the clay can survive being flattened when the statue cannot. The Ship of Theseus asks whether gradual replacement of every plank preserves numerical identity. Philosophers disagree about when parts compose an object, ranging from unrestricted composition to views denying composite material objects in the strict sense.\n\nPersistence sharpens the problem of change. A tree loses leaves and grows while apparently remaining one tree. Endurantism describes it as wholly present at different times; perdurantism treats a persisting thing as having temporal parts. Stage theories offer another account using momentary stages and their continuity relations. These views must distinguish numerical identity, being the very same individual, from qualitative similarity, sharing properties. The problem becomes especially difficult for persons, where memory, psychological continuity and bodily survival can come apart.\n\nModality studies necessity, possibility and contingency. The fact that a book lies on a desk could have been otherwise; whether mathematical or metaphysical truths could have been otherwise raises harder questions. Possible-worlds semantics helps state modal claims precisely, but possible worlds themselves admit rival interpretations, from concrete worlds to abstract representations. Essentialism asks which features something could not lack and still be that thing. Logical, physical and metaphysical possibility need not coincide, and conceivability alone does not settle what is possible.\n\nThe nature of time concerns more than clocks. A-theories give objective significance to temporal becoming or tense, whereas B-theories explain temporal ordering without an objectively privileged moving present. Presentism, eternalism and growing-block theories disagree about what times exist. Special relativity complicates the idea of a universal present, although philosophers debate what follows metaphysically. The persistence debate intersects with these theories because what it means to exist at multiple times depends partly on what times are.\n\nCausation asks why one event produces or brings about another. Hume's account challenges the claim that we observe a necessary connection beyond regular succession. Contemporary theories investigate regularities, counterfactual dependence, causal processes and powers. Preemption cases, in which one cause acts before another potential cause can, challenge simple counterfactual tests. Determinism holds that laws together with a complete state fix subsequent events; it is not fatalism, which treats outcomes as occurring regardless of our actions. Disputes about free will ask whether meaningful agency can coexist with determinism.\n\nMetaphysics extends to minds and societies. Debates over mind and body ask whether mental properties are reducible to physical properties, dependent on them or fundamentally distinct. Social ontology examines money, institutions, laws and socially constructed categories. Social dependence does not imply unreality: a debt or legal status can have real consequences while depending on collective practices. Disputes about race and gender concern the status of social kinds, their relation to biology and history, and whether different classifications explain or obscure important realities.\n\nMetaphysics also faces methodological criticism. Kant argues that theoretical reason exceeds its legitimate reach when it purports to know certain objects beyond possible experience; this differs from simply rejecting all metaphysical inquiry. Carnap and other logical empiricists challenged traditional metaphysical disputes and pressed for clarity about linguistic frameworks and empirical significance. Contemporary defenders appeal to explanatory virtues, conceptual analysis, logic and continuity with science, while critics question whether these methods can choose among empirically equivalent ontologies. The history of the field includes distinct projects, from Aristotle and Spinoza to Kant, Heidegger and process-oriented philosophies: their concepts should be compared without pretending they ask precisely the same questions.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mereology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Mereology" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/possible-worlds/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Possible Worlds" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Time" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/social-ontology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Social Ontology" }] },
  { id: "epistemology", n: "Epistemology", k: "branch", d: "The study of knowledge and justified belief: what knowledge is, where it comes from, and how far it reaches.", q: ["What is knowledge, and how does it differ from true belief?", "Can anything be known with certainty?", "What do experience and reason each contribute?"], p: ["plato", "descartes", "locke", "hume", "kant", "quine", "harding", "fricker"], st: ["Plato, Theaetetus", "Hume, An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748)", "Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781)"], rel: ["metaphysics", "science", "logic", "feminist"], x: "Epistemology studies cognitive success and failure: knowing, understanding, believing with good reason, and their opposites. Its central concept has long been knowledge that something is the case, which differs from knowing a person or knowing how to do something.\n\nThe traditional analysis treated knowledge as justified true belief. Edmund Gettier's three-page paper of 1963 described cases in which someone has a justified true belief that is true only by luck, and so does not seem to know. Responses include adding a fourth condition, accounts built on intellectual virtues, and treating knowledge as basic and unanalysable. Debates about justification divide evidentialists, who tie it to the evidence a person has, from reliabilists, who tie it to reliable belief-forming processes; internalists hold that justification depends only on what is within the believer's own perspective, and externalists deny this. On the structure of justification, foundationalists hold that some beliefs are basic and support the rest, while coherentists hold that beliefs support one another as a web.\n\nThe traditional sources of knowledge are perception, introspection, memory, reason and testimony, the last raising the question of when we may rely on what others tell us. Skeptical arguments, such as the possibility of being a brain in a vat, ask whether any of these sources can deliver knowledge at all. Newer fields include formal epistemology, which models degrees of belief with probability, social epistemology, and feminist epistemology, which studies how a knower's social position and the practices of judging others shape what can be known.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "logic", n: "Logic", k: "branch", d: "The study of correct reasoning: which inferences preserve truth, and why. Modern logic studies formal systems as well as arguments in ordinary language.", q: ["What makes an argument valid?", "What is the logical form of a statement?", "How do logic and language relate?"], p: ["aristotle", "ockham", "frege", "russell", "godel"], st: ["Aristotle, Prior Analytics", "Frege, Begriffsschrift (1879)", "Russell, “On Denoting” (1905)"], rel: ["mathematics", "language", "analytic"], x: "Logic studies correct reasoning, usually in the form of arguments: premises offered in support of a conclusion. Logical consequence is characterized as necessary truth preservation in virtue of form: an argument is valid when it falls under a general scheme every instance of which is valid. Model-theoretic accounts treat validity as the absence of a counterexample, a model in which the premises are true and the conclusion false; proof-theoretic accounts treat it as the existence of a proof. A proof system is sound when everything it proves is valid, and complete when it proves everything valid.\n\nAmpliative inferences go beyond their premises: induction generalizes from observed cases, and abduction, often called inference to the best explanation, infers what would best explain the evidence. Fallacies are bad arguments; the study of them begins with Aristotle's Sophistical Refutations, and formal fallacies, such as affirming the consequent, are distinguished from those that turn on language or context.\n\nAristotle's syllogistic presented valid forms as schemes, and the Stoics, above all Chrysippus, built a logic of propositions on five indemonstrable argument forms. Modern logic transformed the subject. Russell's paradox, discovered around 1901, showed that the naive principle that any condition defines a set leads to contradiction, a blow to Frege's system, and Russell and Whitehead's Principia Mathematica (1910–13) answered it with a theory of types. Gödel proved first-order logic complete in 1929, and his incompleteness theorems (1931) showed that any consistent formal system in which a certain amount of arithmetic can be carried out is incomplete and cannot prove its own consistency. In 1936 Church and Turing independently showed that there can be no decision method for first-order logic; Turing's machines model almost every modern digital computer.\n\nExtended logics apply these tools to necessity and possibility: possible-worlds semantics, with its accessibility relation, yields a whole family of modal systems. Deviant logics give up some classical principles; intuitionistic logic, abstracted from Brouwer's mathematics, rejects excluded middle and double negation elimination.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logical-consequence/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Logical Consequence” (Jc Beall, Greg Restall and Gil Sagi)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/fallacies/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Fallacies” (Hans Hansen)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-ancient/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ancient Logic” (Susanne Bobzien)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell-paradox/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Russell's Paradox” (Andrew David Irvine, Harry Deutsch and Oliver Marshall)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel-incompleteness/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gödel's Incompleteness Theorems” (Panu Raatikainen)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/church-turing/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Church-Turing Thesis” (B. Jack Copeland)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-modal/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Modal Logic” (James Garson)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-intuitionistic/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Intuitionistic Logic” (Joan Moschovakis)" }] },
  { id: "ethics", n: "Ethics", k: "branch", d: "The study of how to live and act: what is good, what is right, and what we owe one another. It divides into metaethics, normative ethics and applied ethics.", q: ["What makes an action right or wrong?", "What is a good life?", "Are there moral facts?"], p: ["aristotle", "kant", "mill", "nietzsche", "beauvoir", "anscombe"], st: ["Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics", "Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785)", "Mill, Utilitarianism (1861)", "Nietzsche, On the Genealogy of Morality (1887)"], rel: ["political", "religion", "law", "chinese"], x: "Ethics asks how to live and what we owe one another. Virtue ethics is one of the three major approaches in normative ethics, alongside deontology and consequentialism: consequentialists judge acts by their outcomes, deontologists hold that some acts are required or forbidden whatever the consequences, and virtue ethicists center on character, following Aristotle.\n\nMetaethics studies the presuppositions of moral thought and talk: what moral words mean, whether moral facts exist, how we could know them, and how moral judgment bears on motivation. Hume's point that an “ought” cannot be derived from an “is”, and Moore's open-question argument, set much of its modern agenda. Non-cognitivists deny that moral judgments state facts: emotivists take them to express emotion, R. M. Hare took them to express prescriptions of a universal sort, and Simon Blackburn's quasi-realism tries to explain why we may nonetheless treat them as true or false. J. L. Mackie, who coined the term “error theory” in 1977, held that moral judgments aim at objective facts but are never true.\n\nApplied ethics takes up particular questions, such as war, punishment, the treatment of animals or the ending of life, and the debate over moral luck asks how far we can be judged for what lies beyond our control.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaethics” (Geoff Sayre-McCord)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ethics-virtue/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Virtue Ethics” (Rosalind Hursthouse and Glen Pettigrove)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-cognitivism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism” (Mark van Roojen)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-anti-realism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Moral Anti-Realism” (Richard Joyce)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-luck/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Moral Luck” (Dana K. Nelkin)" }] },
  { id: "aesthetics", n: "Aesthetics", k: "branch", d: "The study of beauty, art and taste: what art is, how aesthetic judgment works, and what art does in social life.", q: ["What makes something a work of art?", "Can judgments of taste be correct?", "How is art shaped by its social and technical conditions?"], p: ["burke", "kant", "hegel", "benjamin", "adorno", "lyotard"], st: ["Kant, Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790)", "Benjamin, “The Work of Art in the Age of Its Technological Reproducibility” (1935–36)", "Adorno, Aesthetic Theory (1970)"], rel: ["critical-theory", "phenomenology"], x: "The word “aesthetic” entered European philosophy in the eighteenth century, and it now names an object, a judgment, an attitude, an experience and a kind of value. It grew out of the concept of taste, developed partly against rationalist accounts of beauty. Dubos, Hume and Reid held that judgments of beauty are immediate, like judgments of the senses: reasoning may be needed to take in a complex work, but the verdict itself is not inferred.\n\nHutcheson and Hume held that pleasure in beauty is disinterested, and Kant made the idea central: a judgment of taste is contemplative and indifferent to whether its object serves any desire, yet it claims everyone's agreement. Formalists such as Hanslick, Clive Bell and Clement Greenberg held that only a work's formal properties matter to its value as art. Arthur Danto challenged this with objects that look identical to ordinary things, such as Warhol's Brillo Boxes, and Kendall Walton argued that the category under which we perceive a work changes which aesthetic properties it has.\n\nTwentieth-century debates asked whether aesthetic judgments follow general principles (Beardsley) or respond to particulars (Sibley, Isenberg); whether a special aesthetic attitude of disinterested attention exists (Schopenhauer, Bullough, Stolnitz), which George Dickie's critique did much to undermine; and whether aesthetic experience is defined by its inner character (Dewey, early Beardsley) or by the features of what is experienced. A parallel tradition, running from Benjamin to Adorno, asks what art does in social and political life.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-concept/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "political", n: "Political and social philosophy", k: "branch", d: "The study of power, authority, justice and freedom, and of the social structures that shape people's lives: what makes rule legitimate, and what a just society would be.", q: ["What makes political authority legitimate?", "What does justice require?", "How do social structures produce and constrain persons?"], p: ["plato", "hobbes", "locke", "rousseau", "marx", "schmitt", "arendt", "rawls"], st: ["Plato, Republic", "Hobbes, Leviathan (1651)", "Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762)", "Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971)"], rel: ["ethics", "law", "marxism", "critical-theory", "race"], x: "Political philosophy asks on what grounds citizens owe allegiance to political authority, what justice requires, and what freedom is. Isaiah Berlin distinguished negative liberty, freedom from deliberate interference by others, from positive liberty, the power to act and govern oneself.\n\nPlato's Republic asked what justice is in the soul and the city, and Aristotle's Politics what the best constitution would be. Hobbes was one of the first and greatest social contract thinkers; Locke began from a state of perfect freedom; Rousseau's Social Contract made the general will the source of legitimate law; and Mill's On Liberty set a presumption in favor of freedom. Marx made the economic structure of society the basis of its legal and political institutions.\n\nIn the twentieth century liberalism turned to theories of social justice, above all John Rawls's A Theory of Justice (1971), and Robert Nozick's Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) answered that liberty upsets patterns of distribution. Gramsci's concept of hegemony, rule through consent organized across civil society as well as through coercion, became a basic tool of critical theory, and critics of the contract tradition, Carole Pateman and Charles Mills among them, argued that it rested on sexual and racial exclusions.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberalism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Liberalism” (Shane D. Courtland, Gerald Gaus and David Schmidtz)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/gramsci/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Antonio Gramsci” (James Martin)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx” (Jonathan Wolff and David Leopold)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/critical-phil-race/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Critical Philosophy of Race” (Linda Alcoff)" }], img: { file: "Leviathan_frontispiece_cropped_British_Museum.jpg", alt: "Frontispiece of Leviathan: a crowned giant whose body is made of many small figures rises over a town", cap: "Frontispiece of Hobbes's Leviathan (1651), etched by Abraham Bosse: the sovereign's body is made of his subjects. Public domain, via" } },
  { id: "mind", n: "Philosophy of mind", k: "branch", d: "The study of the mind and consciousness, and of their relation to the body and the physical world.", q: ["How is the mind related to the body?", "What is consciousness?", "How can we know other minds?"], p: ["descartes", "elisabeth", "brentano", "ryle", "nagel"], st: ["Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641)", "Ryle, The Concept of Mind (1949)", "Nagel, “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974)"], rel: ["metaphysics", "language", "phenomenology"], x: "Philosophy of mind centers on the relation between mind and body. Descartes argued that because he could clearly and distinctly conceive his mind existing without his body, the two are distinct substances. The leading objection is interaction: mind and body seem to lack what they would need in common to act on each other, an objection Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed in her letters to Descartes. Property dualists hold that there are two essentially different kinds of property, mental and physical, without immaterial substances.\n\nPhysicalism holds that everything is physical, or supervenes on the physical; ancient atomists such as Democritus are usually read as its forerunners. Thomas Nagel argued that physicalism leaves out the subjective character of experience, what it is like to be a creature, and the hardest problems of the field concern this consciousness and the way thoughts are about things.\n\nKripke appealed to rigid designation in arguing against the identity of pain with any brain state, and Gilbert Ryle, in The Concept of Mind (1949), attacked Cartesian dualism as the “official doctrine” of philosophers and theologians.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Dualism” (Ralph Weir)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/physicalism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Physicalism” (Daniel Stoljar)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/consciousness/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Consciousness” (Robert Van Gulick)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/elisabeth-bohemia/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Elisabeth of Bohemia” (Lisa Shapiro)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rigid-designators/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Rigid Designators” (Joseph LaPorte)" }], img: { file: "Descartes_mind_and_body.gif", alt: "Descartes's drawing of the eyes, the nerves and the pineal gland", cap: "Drawing from Descartes's Treatise of Man (1664) of the pineal gland's part in vision. Public domain, via", wide: true } },
  { id: "language", n: "Philosophy of language", k: "branch", d: "The study of meaning, reference and truth, and of what people do with words.", q: ["How do words come to mean anything?", "How do names refer to things?", "What do we do when we speak?"], p: ["frege", "saussure", "wittgenstein", "austin", "kripke"], st: ["Frege, “On Sense and Reference” (1892)", "Wittgenstein, Philosophical Investigations (1953)", "Austin, How to Do Things with Words (1962)"], rel: ["logic", "mind", "analytic", "poststructuralism"], x: "Modern philosophy of language begins with Frege, who distinguished an expression's reference, the thing it picks out, from its sense, the way that thing is presented. The distinction explains why “the morning star is the evening star” can be informative though both names refer to Venus. Russell's “On Denoting” (1905) analyzed phrases like “the present King of France” as claims of existence and uniqueness, so that a sentence about something that does not exist can be meaningful and false.\n\nWittgenstein's Tractatus (1921) held that propositions picture facts, and the Vienna Circle's verificationism counted a statement meaningful only if it is true by definition or can be checked by experience, which ruled out much of metaphysics and ethics. Tarski gave a rigorous definition of truth for formal languages. Quine attacked the distinction between analytic and synthetic truths and argued that translation is indeterminate. The later Wittgenstein of the Philosophical Investigations (1953) tied meaning to use within “language games” embedded in social practice, and argued that a private language is impossible.\n\nLater work includes truth-conditional semantics (Davidson, Lewis); the causal theory of reference, on which Kripke and Putnam held that names and natural-kind terms refer through a chain of use going back to an initial naming, leading to Putnam's claim that meanings are not in the head; inferentialist accounts of meaning (Sellars, Brandom); and speech act theory, in which Austin and Searle treated utterances as actions, while Grice explained how speakers convey more than they literally say. Continental approaches to language, from Saussure to Derrida, appear under structuralism and post-structuralism.", src: "https://iep.utm.edu/lang-phi/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "science", n: "Philosophy of science", k: "branch", d: "The study of what science is, how scientific theories are tested and confirmed, and how science changes over time.", q: ["What separates science from non-science?", "How does evidence support a theory?", "Does science progress toward truth?"], p: ["bacon", "hume", "popper", "kuhn", "haraway"], st: ["Popper, The Logic of Scientific Discovery (1934)", "Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962)", "Haraway, “Situated Knowledges” (1988)"], rel: ["epistemology", "logic", "mathematics"], x: "Philosophy of science asks what distinguishes science from other inquiry, how evidence supports theories, and what science achieves. Karl Popper accepted Hume's critique of induction and held that induction is never actually used in science: science proceeds by conjectures and attempted refutations, and falsifiability marks a theory as scientific.\n\nHume's problem of induction, what justifies beliefs about the unobserved, remains open; Bayesians treat evidence as updating degrees of belief, and others stress inference to the best explanation. Hempel's deductive-nomological model, the most influential twentieth-century account of explanation, treated an explanation as a derivation from laws; asymmetries such as the flagpole and its shadow showed that it misses something, and later accounts stress causal processes or unification. Holism about testing, the Duhem–Quine thesis, holds that hypotheses are tested only in groups: when Uranus's orbit failed to fit Newton's mechanics, astronomers revised a background assumption and found Neptune, though the same move failed for Mercury.\n\nThomas Kuhn's Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962) described normal science as puzzle-solving within a paradigm, broken by revolutions in which a rival paradigm supersedes it, and argued that theory choice is not governed by rules alone and that rival paradigms lack a common measure. Paul Feyerabend's Against Method (1975) denied that any universal method exists. Realists hold that successful theories are approximately true; their critics point to the well-confirmed theories later displaced by rivals no one had yet conceived.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/thomas-kuhn/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Thomas Kuhn” (Alexander Bird)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Popper” (Stephen Thornton)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/induction-problem/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Problem of Induction” (Leah Henderson)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-explanation-20th/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “20th Century Theories of Scientific Explanation” (Lauren Ross)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-underdetermination/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Underdetermination of Scientific Theory” (Kyle Stanford)" }], img: { file: "Houghton_EC.B1328.620ib_-_Novum_organum_scientiarum.jpg", alt: "Engraved title page of Bacon's Novum Organum", cap: "Engraved title page of Bacon's Novum Organum (1620), Houghton Library, Harvard. Public domain, via" } },
  { id: "religion", n: "Philosophy of religion", k: "branch", d: "The rational examination of religious belief and practice: arguments about God's existence, the problem of evil, the sacred, and the relation of faith to reason.", q: ["Can God's existence be proven?", "How can a good God permit evil?", "What is the relation between faith and reason?"], p: ["augustine", "anselm", "aquinas", "ghazali", "hume", "kierkegaard"], st: ["Anselm, Proslogion", "al-Ghazālī, The Incoherence of the Philosophers", "Hume, Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779)", "Kierkegaard, Fear and Trembling (1843)"], rel: ["metaphysics", "ethics", "islamic", "indian"], x: "Philosophy of religion examines religious traditions and wider questions of religious meaning: what religion is, what God might be, and what significance the cosmos and history might have. It draws on every major area of philosophy, and it has increasingly turned to non-theistic and non-Abrahamic traditions such as Buddhism, Hinduism and Daoism.\n\nIn the mid-twentieth century logical positivists such as A. J. Ayer judged religious claims meaningless because they cannot be verified by experience. Critics replied that the criterion of verification undermines itself and is too narrow, and philosophers influenced by Wittgenstein turned to how religious language functions within religious life. In religious epistemology, evidentialists require that belief in God be supported by evidence, while Alvin Plantinga's reformed epistemology holds that such belief can be warranted without argument. Further debates concern disagreement among equally reasonable people, and how science and religion relate.\n\nThe classic arguments for God's existence are the ontological argument, which Anselm drew from the concept of a being than which none greater can be conceived; cosmological arguments from the existence of the world to a first cause; teleological or design arguments from order in nature; and arguments from religious experience. Hume's Dialogues and Kant's first Critique contain the most famous criticisms. Other central topics are the divine attributes, such as omniscience and how it fits with human freedom, and the problem of evil: how a good and all-powerful God could permit suffering.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-religion/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "history", n: "Philosophy of history", k: "branch", d: "Reflection on whether history has a direction or meaning, and on how historical knowledge is possible.", q: ["Does history have a direction?", "What drives historical change?", "How should the past be written?"], p: ["hegel", "marx", "benjamin", "kojeve", "foucault", "fukuyama"], st: ["Hegel, Lectures on the Philosophy of History", "Benjamin, “On the Concept of History” (1940)", "Foucault, “Nietzsche, Genealogy, History” (1971)"], rel: ["marxism", "german-idealism", "poststructuralism"], x: "Philosophy of history asks what history consists of, whether it has a meaning or direction, how historians know and explain the past, and what moral responsibilities the past creates. Its speculative strand looked for large patterns. Vico held that a universal human nature shapes the course of nations, Herder that human nature is itself formed by history, Hegel that history is the unfolding of freedom, and Marx that it is driven by developments in production and class conflict. Critics object to the single causes these accounts propose and to finding purpose where no agent directs events.\n\nThe hermeneutic tradition separated the human sciences from the natural sciences. Dilthey held that the former rest on understanding meaningful action, the latter on causal explanation, and Collingwood proposed that historians know the past by re-enacting the thought of historical agents. Analytic philosophers debated Hempel's covering-law model of 1942, on which historical explanation derives events from general laws; Dray and others argued that history rarely works this way.\n\nFrom the 1970s Hayden White and Louis Mink turned attention to narrative and to the rhetorical form of historical writing, and Ricoeur linked memory, narrative, identity and history. Reinhart Koselleck's conceptual history studies how the concepts historians use change over time. Recent work emphasizes that the historian's choice of theme, place and scale shapes what can be known, that evidence underdetermines narrative so that several truthful accounts can coexist, and that Eurocentric frameworks distort the histories of other parts of the world.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/history/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "law", n: "Philosophy of law", k: "branch", d: "The study of what law is, how it relates to morality, and what justifies punishment.", q: ["What makes a rule a law?", "Must law be just to be law?", "What justifies punishment?"], p: ["aquinas", "bentham", "johnaustin", "kelsen", "hart", "dworkin", "foucault"], st: ["Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I–II, qq. 90–97", "Hart, The Concept of Law (1961)", "Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975)"], rel: ["political", "ethics"], x: "Philosophy of law, or jurisprudence, asks what law is and how it relates to morality. Legal positivists hold that whether a norm is legally valid depends only on social facts, such as whether it was enacted in the right way, and never on its moral merit. John Austin treated law as the command of a sovereign backed by threats. H. L. A. Hart objected that this cannot account for rules that confer powers, such as those for making wills or contracts, and replaced it with a system of primary rules of conduct and secondary rules, including a rule of recognition that officials accept from an internal point of view. Inclusive positivists allow morality into the test of validity when legal convention puts it there; exclusive positivists do not.\n\nNatural law theorists, from Aquinas onward, hold that moral content partly determines what counts as law. Ronald Dworkin argued that identifying the law requires interpreting legal practice in its best moral light, and pointed to disagreements among lawyers as evidence against positivism. Lon Fuller's parable of the inept King Rex illustrated formal requirements, often called the rule of law, that a system must meet to count as law at all.\n\nA further question is law's normativity: why legal requirements give anyone reasons to act. Command theorists appeal to fear of sanctions; Hart argued that officials treat rules as justified standards; Joseph Raz argued that law claims legitimate authority, which is justified when following it helps people act better on reasons they already have. Punishment, its justification and its social function, is a related field, which Foucault approached historically in Discipline and Punish.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lawphil-nature/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "mathematics", n: "Philosophy of mathematics", k: "branch", d: "The study of what mathematical objects are, how mathematical knowledge is possible, and how mathematics relates to logic.", q: ["Do numbers exist?", "How do we know mathematical truths?", "Can mathematics be reduced to logic?"], p: ["plato", "kant", "frege", "russell", "godel"], st: ["Frege, The Foundations of Arithmetic (1884)", "Russell, Introduction to Mathematical Philosophy (1919)"], rel: ["logic", "analytic", "science"], x: "Philosophy of mathematics asks what mathematical objects are and how we can know about them, given that numbers and sets seem not to be physical and mathematical knowledge seems to come from proof alone. Around 1900 three programs tried to secure the foundations of mathematics. Logicism, from Frege and Russell, sought to reduce mathematics to logic; Frege's system fell to Russell's paradox, though a neo-logicism built on Hume's principle has been revived. Intuitionism, from Brouwer, treats mathematics as mental construction, rejects completed infinities and proofs of existence that give no construction, and so revises classical logic. Formalism, from Hilbert, treated higher mathematics as a formal system whose consistency was to be proved; Gödel's incompleteness theorems undermined that hope.\n\nPlatonists hold that mathematical objects exist independently of us. Gödel defended this view, and Quine and Putnam argued that, because our best scientific theories cannot do without mathematics, we are committed to the entities it describes. Paul Benacerraf posed two problems for platonism: how we could know about abstract objects that we cannot causally interact with, and which sets the natural numbers really are, when several set-theoretic constructions serve equally well.\n\nStructuralists such as Shapiro and Resnik answer that mathematics describes structures, positions in patterns, and nominalists, such as Hartry Field, try to do without abstract objects altogether, or treat mathematics as a kind of useful fiction. Current debates concern whether set theory or category theory should serve as the foundation, the status of the continuum hypothesis, computer-assisted proofs such as that of the four-color theorem, and the practice of mathematics as it actually develops.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-mathematics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  // traditions and approaches
  { id: "ancient", n: "Ancient Greek and Roman philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "From the Presocratics through Socrates, Plato and Aristotle to the Hellenistic schools (Stoics, Epicureans, Skeptics). It set many of the questions and terms the later Western tradition works with.", q: ["What is the underlying nature of things?", "What is virtue, and can it be taught?", "How should one live to be free of disturbance?"], p: ["parmenides", "socrates", "plato", "aristotle", "epicurus", "epictetus", "lucretius", "pyrrho", "sextus"], st: ["Plato, Republic", "Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics", "Epictetus, Handbook", "Lucretius, On the Nature of Things"], rel: ["islamic", "german-idealism", "metaphysics", "ethics"], x: "Greek philosophy began early in the sixth century BCE, when Thales, whom Aristotle credits with declaring water the first cause, sought reasoned explanations of nature. Anaximander posited an indefinite originating stuff, the apeiron, and Anaximenes replaced it with air. Heraclitus wrote of a logos that holds forever, Parmenides argued that what is cannot come to be or perish, and Zeno's paradoxes, extending Parmenides' arguments, purported to show that ordinary motion is impossible. Empedocles posited four roots, earth, water, air and fire, Anaxagoras a mixture set in rotation by Mind, and Leucippus and Democritus uncuttable atoms in the void. The Sophists raised ethical and political questions, and Protagoras declared that man is the measure of all things.\n\nSocrates turned philosophy toward ethics and the conduct of life, and Athens executed him for impiety in 399 BCE. Plato's dialogues, most of them led by Socrates, set out the theory of Forms and, in the Republic, a city ruled by philosophers. Aristotle studied in Plato's Academy, founded the Lyceum, criticized the Forms and wrote on logic, physics, biology, ethics, politics and rhetoric.\n\nAfter Alexander the Hellenistic schools offered ways of life: Epicureans joined atomism to the pursuit of tranquility, Stoics taught living in agreement with nature and built a logic of propositions, and Pyrrhonists suspended judgment.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/presocratics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Presocratic Philosophy” (Patricia Curd)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-ancient/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ancient Logic” (Susanne Bobzien)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paradox-zeno/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Zeno's Paradoxes” (Nick Huggett)" }], img: { file: "\"The_School_of_Athens\"_by_Raffaello_Sanzio_da_Urbino.jpg", alt: "Raphael's fresco The School of Athens: philosophers gathered under a vaulted hall", cap: "Raphael, The School of Athens, 1509–1511, a fresco in the Apostolic Palace, Vatican; Plato and Aristotle stand at the center. Public domain, via", wide: true } },
  { id: "german-idealism", n: "German idealism", k: "tradition", d: "The philosophy that developed in response to Kant's critical philosophy, from Fichte and Schelling to Hegel's system, centered on reason, freedom and self-consciousness.", q: ["What are the conditions of possible experience?", "How is self-consciousness structured?", "Can philosophy grasp the whole as a system?"], p: ["kant", "fichte", "schelling", "hegel"], st: ["Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781)", "Hegel, Phenomenology of Spirit (1807)"], rel: ["marxism", "phenomenology", "critical-theory", "history"], x: "German idealism ran from about the 1780s to the 1840s, and its main figures were Kant, Fichte, Schelling and Hegel. Kant held that we know objects only as they appear to us, under the forms of our sensibility and understanding, never as they are in themselves. Early critics read this as a version of Berkeley's idealism, a reading Kant rejected.\n\nReinhold tried to derive Kant's philosophy from a single principle of consciousness. Fichte recast that first principle as an act in which the I posits itself and, in doing so, a not-I opposed to it, and he argued that the idea of a thing in itself is incoherent. Schelling began as Fichte's follower, developed a philosophy of nature, and came to hold that subject and object are identical in an absolute that can be approached from either side. Hegel called Fichte's idealism subjective and Schelling's objective, and sought a position beyond both; his Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) traces consciousness through a series of shapes toward absolute knowing, and his dialectical logic underlies his whole system.\n\nThe movement aimed at a system covering logic, metaphysics, nature, ethics, politics, art and religion, and it gave freedom and self-consciousness a central place. It also gave art a high role in grasping the absolute, though Hegel later held that art had lost its highest vocation. After Hegel's death his followers split into Left and Right Hegelians over the politics of his system, and Schopenhauer, Kierkegaard and Marx attacked it. Its influence faded with the rise of positivism and neo-Kantianism, then revived in the twentieth and twenty-first centuries, in analytic as well as continental philosophy.", src: "https://iep.utm.edu/germidea/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "phenomenology", n: "Phenomenology, hermeneutics and existentialism", k: "tradition", d: "Phenomenology describes the structures of lived experience. Hermeneutics studies interpretation and understanding. Existentialism centers on freedom, choice and concrete existence.", q: ["How is the world given in experience?", "What is it to understand a text, or a life?", "What does freedom demand of us?"], p: ["kierkegaard", "husserl", "heidegger", "sartre", "beauvoir", "merleauponty", "levinas", "gadamer", "ricoeur"], st: ["Husserl, Ideas I (1913)", "Heidegger, Being and Time (1927)", "Sartre, Being and Nothingness (1943)", "Merleau-Ponty, Phenomenology of Perception (1945)", "Gadamer, Truth and Method (1960)"], rel: ["german-idealism", "poststructuralism", "mind", "race"], x: "Phenomenology is the study of the structures of conscious experience as lived from the first-person point of view. Its central concept is intentionality: experience is always of or about something, presented through a meaning. Brentano called the study of mental phenomena descriptive psychology, and Husserl made it a discipline of its own in the Logical Investigations (1900-01) and Ideas I (1913). His method of epoché, or bracketing, sets aside the question whether the world exists in order to describe how things show up in experience. Heidegger's Being and Time (1927) shifted attention to practical involvement in a world and to the question of the meaning of being; Sartre developed an account of consciousness and freedom, and Merleau-Ponty one of the lived body and perception.\n\nHermeneutics is the theory of interpretation. Schleiermacher proposed a general art of interpreting any text, and Dilthey grounded the human sciences in understanding. Heidegger made interpretation a basic feature of human existence. Gadamer's Truth and Method (1960) argued that prejudices, in the sense of prior judgments handed down by tradition, make understanding possible, that we are shaped by an effective history we can never fully survey, and that understanding happens as a fusion of horizons. Habermas objected that this leaves too little ground for criticizing ideology; Ricoeur took a long route to self-understanding through symbol, metaphor and narrative.\n\nExistentialism, a loose movement that flourished in postwar France with roots in Kierkegaard and Nietzsche, starts from concrete individual existence. Only Sartre and Beauvoir accepted the label. Its themes include Sartre's slogan that existence precedes essence, so that people make themselves by their choices; freedom limited by facticity, the conditions one is born into; anxiety; authenticity against conformity to the crowd; and bad faith, the self-deception by which we flee our freedom.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/phenomenology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hermeneutics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hermeneutics”" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/existentialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Existentialism”" }] },
  { id: "marxism", n: "Marxism", k: "tradition", d: "The tradition founded by Marx and Engels: a materialist account of history, a critique of political economy, and a theory of class struggle, developed later by Lukács, Gramsci, Althusser and many others.", q: ["How does capitalism produce and reproduce itself?", "How do ideas relate to material conditions?", "What would emancipation require?"], p: ["marx", "engels", "lukacs", "gramsci", "althusser", "williams"], st: ["Marx and Engels, The Communist Manifesto (1848)", "Marx, Capital, vol. 1 (1867)", "Lukács, History and Class Consciousness (1923)"], rel: ["german-idealism", "critical-theory", "feminist", "race", "postcolonial"], x: "Marxism is the tradition founded by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, who wrote The German Ideology and The Communist Manifesto (1848) together. On historical materialism, forms of society rise and fall as they develop or fetter the productive forces, and political and legal institutions, the superstructure, are explained by the economic structure; the 1859 Preface is the canonical statement. Class conflict is central to Marx's theory of history, and the Manifesto opens: “The history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles.”\n\nUnder capitalism, on Marx's account, profit comes from the surplus labor workers perform beyond the value of their wages, and alienated labor separates workers from the product of their labor, from their productive activity, from one another and from their human nature, or species-being. Marx expected history to move toward a classless communist society, though he deliberately declined to write blueprints for it.\n\nLater Marxism took many forms. Gramsci, usually counted a Western Marxist, made hegemony central; Althusser offered a structuralist reading. Critics such as Robert Nozick argued that if the labor theory of value fails, the theory of exploitation built on it collapses, and Jon Elster criticized the functional explanations of historical materialism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Karl Marx” (Jonathan Wolff and David Leopold)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/gramsci/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Antonio Gramsci” (James Martin)" }] },
  { id: "critical-theory", n: "Frankfurt School critical theory", k: "tradition", d: "Social philosophy from the Institute for Social Research in Frankfurt, combining Marx, Hegel and Freud to criticize modern society with emancipatory aims.", q: ["Why did the promise of enlightenment turn into domination?", "How does mass culture shape consciousness?", "What can critique do?"], p: ["horkheimer", "adorno", "marcuse", "benjamin", "habermas", "honneth"], st: ["Horkheimer, “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937)", "Horkheimer and Adorno, Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/1947)", "Marcuse, One-Dimensional Man (1964)"], rel: ["marxism", "german-idealism", "aesthetics", "poststructuralism"], x: "In its narrow sense, critical theory is the work of the Frankfurt School, the circle around the Institute for Social Research founded in Frankfurt in 1923. In a broader sense the name covers emancipatory theories tied to social movements, such as feminist, critical race, queer and postcolonial theory. Horkheimer's inaugural lecture of 1931 set out an interdisciplinary program joining philosophy with social theory, psychology, political economy and cultural analysis, and his essay “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937) defined critical theory as theory that questions the existing social order and aims at human emancipation. The school drew on Marx, Freud, Weber, Lukács, Kant, Hegel and Nietzsche.\n\nThe first generation included Horkheimer, Adorno, Marcuse and Benjamin. They studied the psychological roots of authoritarianism, culminating in The Authoritarian Personality (1950). Horkheimer and Adorno's Dialectic of Enlightenment (1947) argued that instrumental reason turns enlightenment into new forms of domination and that the culture industry standardizes culture into commodities; Benjamin was more hopeful about mass-reproduced art. Marcuse's One-Dimensional Man (1964) argued that advanced capitalism absorbs dissent. Adorno developed a negative dialectics attentive to what escapes concepts, and defended autonomous art as a form of resistance.\n\nHabermas, leading the second generation, grounded critique in communicative reason: the lifeworld of shared meanings is distorted when money and administrative power colonize it, and in his discourse ethics valid norms are those all affected could accept in rational discussion. Honneth's theory of recognition, building on Hegel, and the work of Benhabib, Fraser and others mark the third generation, which has taken the tradition toward feminist and anti-colonial concerns.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/critical-theory/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "poststructuralism", n: "Structuralism and post-structuralism", k: "tradition", d: "Structuralism explains meaning through systems of differences, from Saussure's linguistics to Lévi-Strauss's anthropology. Post-structuralism, a largely English-language label, covers French thinkers who questioned stable structures, origins and the unified subject.", q: ["How do systems of difference produce meaning?", "How are subjects formed by language, discourse and power?", "What do texts exclude in order to cohere?"], p: ["saussure", "levistrauss", "lacan", "barthes", "althusser", "foucault", "derrida", "kristeva", "lyotard"], st: ["Saussure, Course in General Linguistics (1916)", "Barthes, Mythologies (1957)", "Derrida, Of Grammatology (1967)", "Foucault, Discipline and Punish (1975)"], rel: ["phenomenology", "marxism", "feminist", "postcolonial", "language"], x: "Structuralism explained meaning through systems of relations. Saussure's Course in General Linguistics (1916), compiled from students' notes of his lectures, treated language as a system of signs whose elements have value only through their differences, and Lévi-Strauss in anthropology, Barthes in cultural criticism and Lacan in psychoanalysis, who held that the unconscious is structured like a language, carried the model into their fields.\n\nThe French thinkers who came after, Derrida, Foucault, Deleuze and Lyotard among them, are often called post-structuralists because they worked with concepts developed in the structuralist revolution in Paris in the 1950s and early 1960s while questioning fixed structures, origins and the unified subject; many cite May 1968 as a turning point. Derrida's différance names a play of differences in which no meaning is ever simply present, and his “Structure, Sign, and Play” (1966) took Lévi-Strauss as its main subject. Foucault's genealogy opposes the search for origins and treats knowledge and power together. For Deleuze difference is the principle of genesis. Lyotard's The Postmodern Condition (1979) defined the postmodern as incredulity toward metanarratives, and Barthes's “The Death of the Author” (1967) located a text's meaning in its reading.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/postmodernism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Postmodernism” (Gary Aylesworth)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lacan/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jacques Lacan” (Adrian Johnston)" }, { src: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/literature-and-arts/language-and-linguistics-biographies/ferdinand-de-saussure", srcName: "International Encyclopedia of the Social Sciences, “Saussure, Ferdinand de” (Joseph H. Greenberg)" }] },
  { id: "feminist", n: "Feminist philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "Philosophy that examines gender, sexuality and the oppression of women, and how they shape knowledge, ethics, politics and the self.", q: ["How is gender produced and maintained?", "How does social position shape knowledge?", "What would gender justice require?"], p: ["wollstonecraft", "beauvoir", "hartsock", "harding", "haraway", "butler", "federici", "crenshaw", "fricker"], st: ["Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792)", "Beauvoir, The Second Sex (1949)", "Butler, Gender Trouble (1990)"], rel: ["marxism", "race", "poststructuralism", "epistemology"], x: "Feminism is at once a political movement to end gender-based oppression and a body of concepts and theories for analyzing that oppression. Feminist philosophy brings this commitment to philosophy. Concern for justice for women is much older than any organized movement, and the field's history runs through Mary Astell and Mary Wollstonecraft to Simone de Beauvoir's The Second Sex (1949). Contemporary feminist philosophy emerged in the 1970s, as more women entered universities, and built its own institutions when mainstream journals were slow to accept its work: the Society for Women in Philosophy (1972) and the journal Hypatia (1982) among them.\n\nFeminist philosophers generally treat people as embodied and socially situated, distrust claims to neutrality or universality that may reflect a particular, usually male, standpoint, and study how gender shapes norms and has been used to exclude or subordinate. They work across the discipline: in epistemology and philosophy of science, ethics, political philosophy, metaphysics, aesthetics, philosophy of law, religion and language, and the history of philosophy. Analytic, continental, pragmatist, psychoanalytic and Latin American approaches differ in style and method.\n\nThe distinction between sex and gender has been central to much analytic feminism. Continental feminists have questioned it from two directions: Judith Butler by asking whether sex is itself socially constituted, Luce Irigaray and Hélène Cixous by theorizing sexual difference. Feminist philosophers have also stressed that gender interacts with race, class, disability and sexuality, a point Kimberlé Crenshaw named intersectionality, and debates over defining “woman” now extend to trans and nonbinary people.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/feminist-philosophy/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "race", n: "Philosophy of race and critical race theory", k: "tradition", d: "Inquiry into what race is, how racial categories are made and enforced, and how racism structures law, knowledge and political economy.", q: ["What kind of thing is race?", "How is racism built into institutions?", "How do race, class and gender interlock?"], p: ["dubois", "fanon", "bell", "crenshaw", "omi", "winant", "robinson", "mills"], st: ["Du Bois, The Souls of Black Folk (1903)", "Fanon, Black Skin, White Masks (1952)", "Mills, The Racial Contract (1997)"], rel: ["feminist", "postcolonial", "marxism", "african"], x: "The traditional concept of race divided humanity into a small number of groups defined by inherited biology, with sharp boundaries, geographic origins and physical, sometimes behavioral, markers. Whether the concept is modern is disputed: some trace it to late medieval Spain, to the blood-purity statutes first issued in 1449. Hume, Kant, who described four races, and Blumenbach, who identified five varieties and coined “Caucasian”, contributed to its early modern classifications. Franz Boas showed that cranial size was shaped by environment, and a UNESCO statement of 1950 denied the biological foundations of race.\n\nPhilosophers and scientists now agree in rejecting racial naturalism, the view of discrete, essentialist biological races. Skeptics such as Kwame Anthony Appiah and Naomi Zack have argued that there are no races and the concept should be eliminated; constructivists such as Sally Haslanger hold that races are real as racialized groups made by social practices; others defend population-based conceptions. Further debates concern race and ethnicity, what racism is, and policies such as affirmative action and reparations.\n\nCritical race theory arose in American legal scholarship. Derrick Bell's Race, Racism, and American Law (1973) is among its founding texts, and Kimberlé Crenshaw was a founder of the movement; its ideas include Bell's interest convergence and racial realism, Crenshaw's intersectionality and Cheryl Harris's “whiteness as property” (1993).", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/race/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Race” (Adam Burgos)", srcs: [{ src: "https://law.ucla.edu/faculty/faculty-profiles/kimberle-w-crenshaw", srcName: "UCLA School of Law, “Kimberlé W. Crenshaw” (UCLA School of Law)" }, { src: "https://hls.harvard.edu/today/derrick-bell-1930-2011", srcName: "Harvard Law School, “Derrick Bell, 1930–2011” (Harvard Law School)" }] },
  { id: "postcolonial", n: "Postcolonial and decolonial thought", k: "tradition", d: "Thought about colonialism and its aftermath: how empire shaped knowledge, culture and identity, and what liberation from it involves.", q: ["How did colonialism shape what counts as knowledge?", "Who can speak for the colonized?", "What does decolonization require?"], p: ["cesaire", "fanon", "said", "guha", "spivak", "mbembe"], st: ["Césaire, Discourse on Colonialism (1950)", "Fanon, The Wretched of the Earth (1961)", "Said, Orientalism (1978)", "Spivak, “Can the Subaltern Speak?” (1988)"], rel: ["race", "marxism", "poststructuralism", "african"], x: "Colonialism is a practice of domination in which one people is subjected to another. The term is often used for European settlement, dispossession and rule, and imperialism for domination without large-scale settlement, though the two are often interchanged. Spanish conquest in the Americas was justified by religious mission and papal authority, and challenged on natural-law grounds by Bartolomé de las Casas and Francisco de Vitoria, though Vitoria still allowed force under some conditions. Liberal thinkers such as John Stuart Mill defended despotic rule over peoples they ranked lower on a scale of civilization, and Tocqueville supported French rule in Algeria.\n\nMarx saw British rule in India as destructive but historically progressive; Lenin treated imperialism as a stage of capitalism driven by competition for markets, a line of analysis extended by Luxemburg and later by Wallerstein's world-systems theory. Anti-colonial thinkers wrote from within the struggle. Frantz Fanon analyzed colonial violence, its psychological effects, and the national bourgeoisie that inherits power; Gandhi developed satyagraha and a critique of Western civilization.\n\nPostcolonial theory, especially in literary and cultural studies, grew from Edward Said's Orientalism, which examined how Western knowledge about “the Orient” served power, and from Gayatri Spivak's “Can the Subaltern Speak?”, which asked whether the most marginalized can be represented without being spoken for. Scholars of settler colonialism, many of them Indigenous, such as Glen Coulthard and Leanne Simpson, argue that the language of the postcolonial obscures colonial rule that continues, criticize politics of recognition and reconciliation, and emphasize Indigenous self-determination.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "pragmatism", n: "Pragmatism", k: "tradition", d: "An American tradition that understands meaning and truth through practical consequences and inquiry.", q: ["What difference does a belief make in practice?", "How are truth and successful practice related?", "How should inquiry proceed?"], p: ["peirce", "james", "dewey"], st: ["Peirce, “How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878)", "James, Pragmatism (1907)", "Dewey, Experience and Nature (1925)"], rel: ["analytic", "epistemology", "science"], x: "Pragmatism treats knowing the world as inseparable from acting in it. It began in the United States around 1870, in discussions at the Metaphysical Club in Cambridge, Massachusetts. Charles Sanders Peirce gave it its method in 1878, the pragmatic maxim: to make a concept clear, trace the practical consequences of its applying. He held that inquiry is always fallible and that truth is what inquiry would arrive at if pursued far enough, and he later renamed his view “pragmaticism” to separate it from James's.\n\nWilliam James popularized pragmatism in 1907, using it to settle or dissolve disputes such as that between free will and determinism, and treated truth as what works in experience, a view critics found too permissive. John Dewey understood inquiry as the response to a problematic situation, and connected it to education, democracy and art. Jane Addams, W. E. B. Du Bois, Alain Locke and George Herbert Mead carried pragmatist ideas into social reform, race, value and the social self.\n\nFrom the 1970s neo-pragmatists such as Richard Rorty, Hilary Putnam, Robert Brandom and Jürgen Habermas revived the tradition, often through language and anti-representationalism, and new pragmatists such as Cheryl Misak sought to restore its classical concern for objectivity. Common themes are fallibilism in place of skepticism, concepts and theories treated as tools judged by their results, and suspicion of sharp dichotomies such as fact and value or theory and practice.\n\nSeveral texts define the tradition. Peirce's “The Fixation of Belief” (1877) treats doubt as an irritation that drives inquiry and compares four ways of settling belief, by tenacity, authority, a priori reasoning and the method of science, preferring the last. James's “The Will to Believe” (1896) argues that when a choice between hypotheses is live, forced and momentous and the evidence cannot decide it, we may let our passional nature decide. Dewey's Logic: The Theory of Inquiry (1938) replaces truth with “warranted assertibility”, and Rorty's Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature (1979) attacked the picture of the mind as a mirror that represents reality, a book that did much to revive interest in the classical pragmatists.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pragmatism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" },
  { id: "analytic", n: "Analytic philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "The tradition, dominant in English-speaking universities, that began with logic and the analysis of language, and prizes argumentative clarity.", q: ["What do our sentences really say?", "Can philosophical problems be solved by clarifying language?", "How does philosophy relate to science?"], p: ["frege", "russell", "moore", "wittgenstein", "carnap", "quine"], st: ["Russell, “On Denoting” (1905)", "Wittgenstein, Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921)", "Quine, “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951)"], rel: ["logic", "language", "pragmatism", "science"], x: "Analytic philosophy originated around the turn of the twentieth century, when G. E. Moore and Bertrand Russell broke away from British absolute idealism; many also count Gottlob Frege as a founder, though some argue he was more an influence. Moore defended common sense, and Russell's theory of descriptions was called a “paradigm of philosophy”. Wittgenstein's Tractatus (1921) was the most influential work of logical atomism.\n\nThe logical positivists of the Vienna Circle, among them Schlick, Neurath and Carnap, held a verification theory of meaning on which much traditional philosophy consisted of meaningless pseudo-problems; several emigrated to the United States, and Ayer's Language, Truth and Logic (1936) brought the view to English readers. At Oxford, Ryle, Austin, Strawson and Grice turned to ordinary language, and the later Wittgenstein held that meaning is use.\n\nQuine's “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” attacked the analytic–synthetic distinction. As the theories of meaning that had banned metaphysics collapsed, and with Kripke's and Ruth Barcan Marcus's work on direct reference, metaphysics returned to the center. Analytic philosophy cannot be defined by a common set of views; it is loosely characterized by its style.", src: "https://iep.utm.edu/analytic-philosophy/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Analytic Philosophy” (Aaron Preston)" },
  { id: "african", n: "African philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "Philosophy from African traditions and thinkers, including debates over what African philosophy is, and work on personhood, community and knowledge.", q: ["What makes a philosophy African?", "What is a person, and what does a person owe the community?", "How should philosophy relate to traditional thought?"], p: ["tempels", "senghor", "hountondji", "wiredu", "oruka", "gyekye", "menkiti"], st: ["Hountondji, African Philosophy: Myth and Reality (1976)", "Wiredu, Philosophy and an African Culture (1980)", "Gyekye, An Essay on African Philosophical Thought (1987)"], rel: ["postcolonial", "race", "ethics"], x: "African philosophy draws on African systems of thought, transmitted largely orally, and on the work of individual thinkers.\n\nTwentieth-century debate turned on what African philosophy is. Placide Tempels's Bantu Philosophy (1945) presented the shared worldview of a people as a philosophy, an approach Paulin Hountondji made notorious as ethnophilosophy, faulting Tempels and his followers for casting African philosophy as an anonymous system of thought without individual thinkers. Henry Odera Oruka's sage philosophy answered the same mistake by seeking the critical reflection of individual sages within traditional communities.\n\nRecurring themes include personhood and community. Ifeanyi Menkiti argued that personhood is attained through participation in communal life, and Kwame Gyekye, who held that human beings are social by nature, counted individual liberty among the basic goods of a society while holding that duties take precedence over rights. Kwasi Wiredu called for the conceptual decolonization of African philosophy: concepts absorbed through colonial languages should be tested against intuitions in one's own language and kept or discarded on their merits.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-sage/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Sage Philosophy” (Dismas Masolo)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-ethics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Ethics” (Kwame Gyekye)" }, { src: "https://iep.utm.edu/wiredu/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Kwasi Wiredu (1931–2022)” (Sanya Osha, Tshwane University of Technology)" }] },
  { id: "chinese", n: "Chinese philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "Confucian, Daoist, Mohist, Legalist, Buddhist and Neo-Confucian thought, much of it concerned with ethics, government and harmony with the dao, or way.", q: ["How does one become a good person?", "What is the dao?", "How should a ruler govern?"], p: ["confucius", "mencius", "xunzi", "laozi", "zhuangzi"], st: ["Confucius, Analects", "Laozi, Daodejing", "Zhuangzi"], rel: ["ethics", "metaphysics"], x: "Classical Chinese philosophy took shape in the Warring States era (479–221 BCE), when rival schools competed for the attention of rulers. Confucius sought to preserve the Zhou ritual system and taught the way of the gentleman (junzi), with its virtues, among them ren, benevolence. His followers Mencius and Xunzi disagreed over how far goodness is rooted in natural dispositions and how far it is the product of training. Mozi argued for jian ai, inclusive care, and the Mohists debated the Confucians; Daoist texts such as the Daodejing and Zhuangzi attacked the authoritarian views of Mohism, Xunzi and Han Fei, the leading figure of the school later called fa jia, or Legalism.\n\nBy the Han dynasty experts in the Confucian classics held scholastic positions in the government. Much later, Zhu Xi (1130–1200) gave the Analects a Neo-Confucian interpretation and made it part of a curriculum built on the Four Books.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mohism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Mohism” (Chris Fraser)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Confucius” (Mark Csikszentmihalyi)" }] },
  { id: "indian", n: "Indian philosophy", k: "tradition", d: "The classical Indian schools, Hindu, Buddhist and Jain, which debated the self, consciousness, knowledge and liberation with highly developed epistemology and logic.", q: ["Is there an enduring self?", "What are the valid sources of knowledge?", "What is liberation, and how is it reached?"], p: ["nagarjuna", "shankara", "ramanuja"], st: ["The Upanishads", "The Bhagavad Gītā", "Nāgārjuna, Mūlamadhyamakakārikā"], rel: ["metaphysics", "religion", "logic"], x: "The classical systems (darśanas) of the Indian subcontinent include the Vedic schools, Nyāya, Vaiśeṣika, Sāṃkhya, Yoga, Mīmāṃsā and Vedānta, and the Buddhist, Jain and Cārvāka traditions, which reject Vedic authority. They developed elaborate theories of the valid means of knowledge (pramāṇas): perception, inference and testimony above all, with analogy accepted by some schools, often in the service of liberation from the world.\n\nBuddhists deny that any of the psychophysical aggregates is a self, and argued with Brahmanical schools, such as Sāṃkhya and Nyāya, that defended an enduring self. Nāgārjuna's Madhyamaka turned a skeptical critique on the whole pramāṇa program, and Yogācāra developed an idealist epistemology. Within Vedānta, Śaṅkara's Advaita holds that the self and ultimate reality, brahman, are not two.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology-india/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Epistemology in Classical Indian Philosophy” (Anand Vaidya)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/buddha/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Buddha” (Mark Siderits)" }] },
  { id: "islamic", n: "Philosophy in the Islamic world", k: "tradition", d: "Philosophy written mainly in Arabic and Persian that took up and transformed Greek thought, debating God, the intellect and the relation of reason to revelation.", q: ["How do reason and revelation relate?", "Is the world eternal or created?", "What is the intellect?"], p: ["farabi", "ibnsina", "ghazali", "ibnrushd"], st: ["Ibn Sīnā, The Book of Healing", "al-Ghazālī, The Incoherence of the Philosophers", "Ibn Rushd, The Incoherence of the Incoherence"], rel: ["ancient", "religion", "metaphysics"], x: "Philosophy in the Islamic world developed along two currents. Falsafa took up Aristotle and the Neoplatonists in Arabic translation, and kalām was rational theology; philosophers such as al-Fārābī, Ibn Sīnā and Ibn Rushd regarded kalām as unscientific dialectical argument.\n\nThe first explicit occasionalists were theologians of the Ashʿarite school of kalām. Al-Ghazālī's Incoherence of the Philosophers, directed chiefly against Ibn Sīnā, argued that we observe only that burning follows the contact of fire with cotton, never a necessary connection, so that God creates the burning each time. Ibn Rushd replied in The Incoherence of the Incoherence that things have essences that determine their functions, and his commentaries on Aristotle, among them the long commentary on De anima, were transmitted to Jewish and Latin readers.\n\nPhilosophy continued in the east, in the illuminationist philosophy of Suhrawardī and later in Mullā Ṣadrā.", src: "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/new-cambridge-history-of-islam/philosophy/845F85110D771F505FD5AC7C0AB015FB", srcName: "The New Cambridge History of Islam, vol. 4 (2010), ch. 21, “Philosophy” (Richard C. Taylor)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/occasionalism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Occasionalism” (Sukjae Lee)" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-rushd/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ibn Rushd [Averroes]” (Robert Pasnau)" }] },
];

// Reading paths through the classics that come with the app (data/books/). Each step names a book by its id, the chapters to read
// (its section ids, in order), what they do (why) and questions to read them with (q); terms are glossary entries to look at along the
// way; next points beyond the app's books.
// A path's br lists the branches and traditions it belongs to; their entries offer it. ws: a Wikisource search (lang, q) for its next reading.
const PATHS = [
  { id: "how-to-live", n: "How should one live?", br: ["ethics"],
    d: "Classic answers, each taking up the ones before it: Socrates' examined life, the Republic's question whether justice pays, Aristotle's virtues, Kant's duty, and Nietzsche's attack on morality itself.",
    steps: [
      { book: "classic-plato-socrates", secs: ["classic-plato-socrates-s2", "classic-plato-socrates-s3"], why: "Socrates at his trial, defending a life spent questioning others, then in prison refusing to escape his sentence. Short, and the usual starting point for Western ethics.", q: ["In the Apology, what does Socrates take the oracle's saying that no one is wiser than he to mean, and how has it shaped the way he lives?", "Why does Socrates say the unexamined life is not worth living? What would an examined life involve?", "In the Crito, what reasons do the Laws give Socrates for staying in prison? Which is the strongest, and which the weakest?"] },
      { book: "classic-plato-republic", secs: ["classic-plato-republic-s1", "classic-plato-republic-s2"], why: "Books I and II: Thrasymachus declares that justice is nothing else than the interest of the stronger. When Socrates has silenced him, Glaucon and Adeimantus renew the challenge, with the story of Gyges' ring among it: show that justice is worth having for its own sake as well as for its rewards.", q: ["What exactly does Thrasymachus claim justice is, and how does Socrates try to show the claim undoes itself?", "Glaucon sorts goods into three classes. Where does common opinion put justice, and where does Socrates want to put it?", "What is the story of Gyges' ring meant to show? Would you act differently if no one could ever see you?"] },
      { book: "classic-aristotle-ethics", secs: ["classic-aristotle-ethics-s2", "classic-aristotle-ethics-s3"], why: "Books I and II: the human good is happiness (eudaimonia), an activity of the soul in accordance with virtue, and a virtue of character is a settled state, lying in a mean between extremes, acquired by practice.", q: ["Why does Aristotle think there must be a highest good for which everything else is chosen, and why does he call it happiness?", "Aristotle asks what the function of man is. What does his answer tell him the human good must be?", "How, according to Book II, do we come to have virtues of character, and what does it mean that virtue lies in a mean?"] },
      { book: "classic-kant-groundwork", secs: ["classic-kant-groundwork-s2", "classic-kant-groundwork-s3"], why: "The First and Second Sections: only a good will is good without qualification, and morality's supreme principle is a categorical imperative, binding on every rational being whatever they happen to want.", q: ["Why does Kant say that nothing can be called good without qualification except a good will? Test the claim against his examples.", "What is the difference between acting in accordance with duty and acting from duty? How does the dealer who doesn't overcharge show it?", "Take one of Kant's four examples in the Second Section. Does the maxim really fail to become a universal law?"] },
      { book: "classic-nietzsche-genealogy", secs: ["classic-nietzsche-genealogy-s2", "classic-nietzsche-genealogy-s3"], why: "The Preface and First Essay: Nietzsche asks what our moral values are worth, and traces the opposition of good and evil back to an older one of good and bad, overturned in what he calls the slave revolt in morality.", q: ["What question about the value of morality does Nietzsche set himself in the Preface?", "How does the opposition of good and bad differ from that of good and evil, and from whose point of view is each made?", "What does Nietzsche say resentment does when it becomes creative?"] },
    ],
    terms: ["eudaimonia", "virtue-ethics", "deontology", "categorical-imperative", "autonomy", "ressentiment", "genealogy"],
    next: "Mill's Utilitarianism (1861), the classic statement of the view this path leaves out: that right actions are those that tend to promote happiness.",
    ws: { lang: "en", q: "Utilitarianism Mill" } },
  { id: "what-can-we-know", n: "What can we know?", br: ["epistemology"],
    d: "Reason against experience. Descartes doubts all he can and rebuilds on what survives; Hume asks what experience can justify, and finds it less than we assume; Russell names what our reasoning from experience rests on, and asks how anything can be known before experience.",
    steps: [
      { book: "classic-descartes-meditations", secs: ["classic-descartes-meditations-s11", "classic-descartes-meditations-s12"], why: "Meditation I doubts everything that can be doubted, down to the possibility of dreaming and of a deceiving demon; Meditation II finds what doubt cannot touch, that he exists while he thinks, and asks what this thinking thing is.", q: ["Why does Descartes doubt the senses, then whether he is awake, then even arithmetic and geometry? What does each new ground of doubt add?", "What does Descartes find that cannot be doubted, even supposing he is deceived about everything else?", "What does the piece of wax show about how we know bodies?"] },
      { book: "classic-hume-enquiry", secs: ["classic-hume-enquiry-s2", "classic-hume-enquiry-s4", "classic-hume-enquiry-s5", "classic-hume-enquiry-s7"], why: "Sections II, IV, V and VII: every idea is copied from an impression; no reasoning shows that the future will resemble the past, so our expectations rest on custom; and we never observe a necessary connection between cause and effect.", q: ["What is the difference between impressions and ideas, and how does Hume use the question “from what impression is that supposed idea derived?”", "Why can't the supposition that the future will resemble the past be proved, either by demonstration or from experience?", "If not reasoning, what does Hume say carries us from past to future in Section V? Is that a sceptical answer, or a solution?"] },
      { book: "classic-russell-problems", secs: ["classic-russell-problems-s7", "classic-russell-problems-s8", "classic-russell-problems-s9"], why: "Chapters VI to VIII: our expectation that the sun will rise tomorrow rests on the principle of induction, which experience can neither prove nor disprove, so we must accept it on its intrinsic evidence or forgo all justification of our expectations about the future. Russell then asks how such general principles can be known before experience, sets out Kant's answer, and argues that it fails.", q: ["What is the man who feeds the chicken every day meant to show about expectations from experience?", "State the principle of induction in your own words. Why does Russell say experience can neither prove nor disprove it?", "How does Kant explain a priori knowledge, as Russell presents him, and what is Russell's main objection?"] },
    ],
    terms: ["rationalism", "empiricism", "skepticism", "dualism", "induction-problem", "a-priori", "synthetic-a-priori"],
    next: "Kant's Critique of Pure Reason (1781), whose answer Russell sets out and rejects in Chapter VIII, is the way into the German philosophy that follows; it isn't among the app's books.",
    ws: { lang: "en", q: "Critique of Pure Reason" } },
  { id: "why-obey", n: "Why obey the state?", br: ["political"],
    d: "The social contract, from Hobbes's absolute sovereign to Rousseau's general will; Mill on the limits of any power, a majority's included, over the individual; then two challenges to the contract's picture of who the citizens are: Wollstonecraft on women, Marx and Engels on class.",
    steps: [
      { book: "classic-hobbes-leviathan", secs: ["classic-hobbes-leviathan-s14", "classic-hobbes-leviathan-s15", "classic-hobbes-leviathan-s16", "classic-hobbes-leviathan-s17", "classic-hobbes-leviathan-s18"], why: "Chapters XIII–XVII: without a common power to keep them in awe, people live in a war of every one against every one; the laws of nature, found by reason, lead them to hand their right to govern themselves to a sovereign.", q: ["What leads people without a common power to a war of every man against every man, and what does Hobbes say life is like there?", "What is the first law of nature, “to seek peace, and follow it,” and how does Hobbes get from it to the second?", "Why does Hobbes say covenants without the sword are but words, and what follows for the sovereign's powers?"] },
      { book: "classic-rousseau-social-contract", secs: ["classic-rousseau-social-contract-s3", "classic-rousseau-social-contract-s4"], why: "Books I and II: each gives himself wholly to all, so that obeying the law one has made oneself is freedom; sovereignty is the general will, which cannot be handed over or divided.", q: ["Rousseau writes that man is born free and is everywhere in chains, and asks what can make that legitimate. Why that question and not another?", "What does each person give up in the social compact, and what does each get in return?", "How does the general will differ from the will of all, and in what sense does Rousseau say it is always right?"] },
      { book: "classic-mill-liberty", secs: ["classic-mill-liberty-s2"], why: "Chapter I: Mill's subject is the nature and limits of the power society can legitimately exercise over the individual. Majorities can be tyrants too, through opinion as well as law, and the only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over anyone against their will is to prevent harm to others.", q: ["Why does Mill think people still need protection from power once their rulers are elected?", "State the harm principle in your own words. Which reasons for interfering with someone does it rule out?", "To whom does Mill say the principle doesn't apply? What do you make of those exceptions?"] },
      { book: "classic-wollstonecraft-vindication", secs: ["classic-wollstonecraft-vindication-s2", "classic-wollstonecraft-vindication-s3", "classic-wollstonecraft-vindication-s4"], why: "The Introduction and Chapters I–II: the rights claimed for men belong to women too, since virtue rests on reason and women share it; their apparent weakness comes from an education that trains them to please. Chapter V takes on Rousseau's picture of women in Émile.", q: ["What does Wollstonecraft blame for women's weakness, and why does she deny that it is natural?", "Why does she ground rights in reason and virtue, and what follows for women?", "Whose views of women is she answering in Chapter II (Milton, Rousseau, Dr. Gregory), and what does she object to in them?"] },
      { book: "classic-marx-engels-manifesto", secs: ["classic-marx-engels-manifesto-s3", "classic-marx-engels-manifesto-s4"], why: "Sections I and II: the history of society is the history of class struggles; the bourgeoisie has remade the world and, in doing so, created the class that will overthrow it.", q: ["What do Marx and Engels mean when they say the history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles?", "What, by their account, has the bourgeoisie achieved, and why do they say it produces its own grave diggers?", "Section II answers objections to abolishing bourgeois property. Pick one objection and judge the reply."] },
    ],
    terms: ["state-of-nature", "social-contract", "sovereignty", "general-will", "harm-principle", "historical-materialism", "ideology"],
    next: "Locke's Second Treatise of Government (1689), the social contract of natural rights and government by consent, between Hobbes and Rousseau.",
    ws: { lang: "en", q: "Second Treatise of Government" } },
  { id: "who-counts", n: "Who counts?", br: ["feminist", "race"],
    d: "Three writers who took their age's talk of rights and progress and asked whom it left out.",
    steps: [
      { book: "classic-wollstonecraft-vindication", secs: ["classic-wollstonecraft-vindication-s2", "classic-wollstonecraft-vindication-s6"], why: "The Introduction and Chapter IV: women are kept in a state of degradation by being denied the education of reason, and the cost falls on society as a whole.", q: ["What does Wollstonecraft say the education of her day trains women for, and at what cost to them?", "In Chapter IV, how does she connect women's dependence with what she calls their degradation?", "Which of her arguments would apply to anyone denied an education, and which are about women in particular?"] },
      { book: "classic-cooper-voice", secs: ["classic-cooper-voice-s1", "classic-cooper-voice-s2"], why: "Cooper's opening essays: Black women's education and standing are the measure of the progress of their race, and they speak from a place that neither white women nor Black men can speak for.", q: ["Cooper calls the Black woman the mute and voiceless note in the debate on race. What does she think that voice can add?", "What does Cooper mean by saying that only the Black woman can say “when and where I enter”? What would that make the measure of progress?", "How does Cooper tie the education of women to the progress of the race as a whole?"] },
      { book: "classic-du-bois-souls", secs: ["classic-du-bois-souls-s1", "classic-du-bois-souls-s2", "classic-du-bois-souls-s4"], why: "The Forethought, Chapter I and Chapter III: the problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the color line; life behind the veil and double consciousness; and Du Bois's break with Booker T. Washington.", q: ["What does Du Bois call the problem of the twentieth century in the Forethought?", "Describe double-consciousness in your own words. What does Du Bois say it costs?", "What does Du Bois object to in Booker T. Washington's program, and what does he grant him?"] },
    ],
    terms: ["double-consciousness", "intersectionality", "standpoint", "matrix-of-domination", "racial-formation"],
    next: "Frederick Douglass's speech “What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?” (1852), on what the nation's founding ideals meant to the people they excluded.",
    ws: { lang: "en", q: "What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July" } },
];

// Disputations: an argument from one of the classics, to answer with your own strongest objection before comparing it with the classic one.
// book and sec are a classic's id and section; find is words in that passage, to open the reader there. The classic objection (x) is
// paraphrased from its sources (src, srcs). label says whether it is an objection or a reply.
const DISPUTES = [
  { id: "euthyphro", n: "Is the pious loved because it is pious?", book: "classic-plato-socrates", sec: "classic-plato-socrates-s1", find: "beloved by the gods because it is holy, or holy because it is beloved", where: "Plato, Euthyphro 10a–11b", p: ["socrates", "plato"], terms: [], prem: ["Euthyphro defines the pious as what all the gods love.", "Socrates asks whether the pious is loved by the gods because it is pious, or pious because it is loved.", "Euthyphro grants the first: the gods love it because it is pious.", "Then being loved by the gods is something that happens to the pious, not what makes it pious."], concl: "So “loved by the gods” can't be what the pious is.", label: "A well-known reply", who: "Robert Adams, and divine command theorists after him", x: "The dilemma has been pressed ever since against grounding morality in God's will: either God commands things because they are right, so rightness doesn't depend on the command, or they are right because commanded, so morality is arbitrary. Adams's reply restricts the theory: obligation is constituted by the commands of a God whose character is good (just and benevolent), while goodness is understood through God's character and not through commands. The commands then aren't arbitrary, and God's goodness isn't merely God meeting a standard of God's own making.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/voluntarism-theological/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Theological Voluntarism” (Mark Murphy), §§2.2, 3.1–3.2" },
  { id: "cartesian-circle", n: "Does God guarantee what I clearly perceive?", book: "classic-descartes-meditations", sec: "classic-descartes-meditations-s14", find: "must of necessity have God for its author", where: "Descartes, Meditations III–V", p: ["descartes"], terms: ["skepticism", "begging"], prem: ["Whatever I perceive clearly and distinctly is true, because God exists and is no deceiver (Meditations IV and V).", "God's existence is proved in Meditation III from premises I perceive clearly and distinctly."], concl: "So clear and distinct perception is reliable, and knowledge is secured.", label: "The classic objection", who: "Marin Mersenne and Antoine Arnauld, in the Second and Fourth Objections (1641)", x: "The argument runs in a circle, the Cartesian circle: we can be sure God exists only because we clearly and distinctly perceive the proof, and sure that clear and distinct perception is reliable only because God exists. Descartes replied that God is needed to vouch for conclusions we remember having proved once we no longer attend to the proof, and not for what we perceive clearly while attending to it (compare the passage in Meditation V about recalling a proof without its grounds). Whether that reply works is still debated; most Descartes scholars, on textual grounds, deny that he argued in a vicious circle.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes-epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Descartes' Epistemology” (Lex Newman)" },
  { id: "elisabeth", n: "How does a thinking thing move a body?", book: "classic-descartes-meditations", sec: "classic-descartes-meditations-s16", find: "entirely and truly distinct from my body", where: "Descartes, Meditation VI", p: ["descartes", "elisabeth"], terms: ["dualism"], prem: ["I have a clear and distinct idea of myself as a thinking, unextended thing.", "I have a clear and distinct idea of body as an extended, unthinking thing.", "What I can clearly and distinctly conceive apart, God can make exist apart."], concl: "So my mind is really distinct from my body, and can exist without it. (Yet the same Meditation says mind and body are so closely joined that they compose a unity.)", label: "The classic objection", who: "Elisabeth of Bohemia, in her letters to Descartes (1643)", x: "Elisabeth asked how a soul that is only a thinking thing could move the body, when the accounts of causation available tied the power to move things to extension. She asked Descartes to explain either the kind of causation that could join an unextended mind to an extended body, or the nature of the soul that made it possible. His replies, which compared the mind's action to the Scholastic idea of heaviness, did not satisfy her, and the question of mind–body interaction has stayed the standing problem for dualism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/elisabeth-bohemia/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Elisabeth of Bohemia” (Lisa Shapiro)" },
  { id: "tacit-consent", n: "Does living here mean I consent?", book: "classic-rousseau-social-contract", sec: "classic-rousseau-social-contract-s6", find: "residence constitutes consent", where: "Rousseau, The Social Contract IV.2", p: ["rousseau", "hume"], terms: ["social-contract"], prem: ["Once the state is instituted, living in its territory counts as consent: “residence constitutes consent.”", "Consent is what gives the state's laws their authority over a person."], concl: "So whoever lives in the state is bound by its laws.", label: "The classic objection", who: "David Hume, “Of the Original Contract” (1748)", x: "Hume's essay, published fourteen years before Rousseau's book, is the classic objection to tacit consent. Most people can't leave the country they were born in: they lack the language, the means and the knowledge to live elsewhere, so staying is no free choice. “We may as well assert, that a man, by remaining in a vessel, freely consents to the dominion of the master; though he was carried on board while asleep, and must leap into the ocean, and perish, the moment he leaves her.” Besides, people born under a government believe they owe it allegiance by birth, not by any choice of theirs.", src: "https://www.carneades.pomona.edu/2024f-Political/13.HumeConsent.handout.pdf", srcName: "Hume, “Of the Original Contract”, arguments against tacit consent (course handout, Pomona College)" },
  { id: "forced-free", n: "Can someone be forced to be free?", book: "classic-rousseau-social-contract", sec: "classic-rousseau-social-contract-s3", find: "he will be forced to be free", where: "Rousseau, The Social Contract I.7", p: ["rousseau"], terms: ["general-will", "autonomy"], prem: ["In the social compact each gives himself wholly to the community, which forms a general will.", "Whoever refuses to obey the general will shall be compelled to obey it by the whole body.", "Obeying the general will is obeying a law one has given oneself."], concl: "So the one compelled “will be forced to be free.”", label: "The classic objection", who: "Isaiah Berlin, “Two Concepts of Liberty” (1958)", x: "Berlin's critique of “positive” liberty, freedom as self-mastery, is the classic statement of the worry, and he counted Rousseau among its theorists. Divide the self into a higher, rational self and a lower one; let those who claim to know your rational interests speak for that higher self; identify it with a collective whole such as the state: then people can be coerced, even oppressed, in the name of their “real” selves, and the coercion called freedom.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/liberty-positive-negative/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Positive and Negative Liberty”" },
  { id: "false-promise", n: "Why can't I make a false promise?", book: "classic-kant-groundwork", sec: "classic-kant-groundwork-s3", find: "promise to repay it, although I know that I never can do so", where: "Kant, Groundwork, Second Section", p: ["kant", "hegel"], terms: ["categorical-imperative", "deontology"], prem: ["Act only on a maxim you could will to be a universal law.", "My maxim: when I need money, I will promise to repay it, knowing I never can.", "Made universal, no one would believe such promises, so promising, and with it my maxim, would be impossible."], concl: "So making a false promise is contrary to duty.", label: "The classic objection", who: "G. W. F. Hegel, Philosophy of Right §135 (1820), the “empty formalism” objection", x: "The test of universalizability yields only formal consistency, so it can't by itself say what our duties are. A contradiction appears only against something already presupposed, that promising (or property) should exist; a world without property contains no more contradiction than one with it. Since the bare form fits one content as well as its opposite, the test can approve wrong maxims as easily as forbid them. Kantians have answered in several ways, and the debate continues.", src: "https://core-cms.cambridgecore.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/45E26FB48598BE5E43EA2EDAA9476FC8/S0263523200000215a.pdf/empty-useless-and-dangerous-recent-kantian-replies-to-the-empty-formalism-objection.pdf", srcName: "Fabian Freyenhagen, “Empty, Useless, and Dangerous? Recent Kantian Replies to the Empty Formalism Objection”, Bulletin of the Hegel Society of Great Britain 63 (2011)" },
  { id: "induction", n: "Is reasoning from experience founded on reason?", book: "classic-hume-enquiry", sec: "classic-hume-enquiry-s4", find: "suppose, as their foundation, that the future will resemble the past", where: "Hume, Enquiry IV", p: ["hume"], terms: ["induction-problem", "induction"], prem: ["Reasoning about matters of fact beyond what we observe rests on cause and effect, known only from experience.", "All inferences from experience suppose that the future will resemble the past.", "That can't be proved by demonstration, since a change in the course of nature implies no contradiction; nor by experience, without arguing in a circle."], concl: "So our inferences from experience are not founded on reasoning.", label: "A classic reply", who: "P. F. Strawson, Introduction to Logical Theory (1952)", x: "Asking for a justification of induction as such misunderstands what justification is. A particular inference from experience is justified when it meets our inductive standards, many and varied instances among them; asking whether those standards themselves are reasonable is like asking whether the legal system as a whole is legal. Being reasonable about matters of fact just means proportioning belief to the evidence by those standards. Critics such as BonJour answer that this shows only that induction conforms to our standards, not that its conclusions are likely to be true, which was Hume's question.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/induction-problem/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Problem of Induction” (Leah Henderson), §5.2" },
];

// Primers: what each classic is, how it is built and words to know, shown on its page before you start. about and built are drawn from the
// sources (src, srcs) and the books themselves; terms are glossary entries.
const PRIMERS = {
  "classic-plato-socrates": { about: "Four dialogues about the last days of Socrates, who was tried in Athens in 399 BCE on charges of irreverence toward the gods (asebeia) and of corrupting the young, and was put to death by drinking hemlock. Euthyphro meets him outside the court where he is to answer the charges; the Apology is his speech at the trial; in the Crito a friend urges him to escape from prison; the Phaedo tells of his last conversation and his death.", built: "Four dialogues, each with Jowett's introduction before it, then the notes.", terms: ["dualism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socrates/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Socrates” (Debra Nails and S. Sara Monoson)" },
  "classic-plato-republic": { about: "A dialogue Socrates narrates the day after it took place. Asked to show that the just life is better than the unjust, and that justice is worth having for its own sake, he sketches a city, where justice is easier to see, then finds the same pattern in the soul: reason, spirit and appetite, with reason ruling.", built: "Ten books. Books V to VII bring in the philosopher-rulers, the form of the good and the cave; Books VIII and IX argue that the just life is better; Book X turns on the poets. Jowett's long introduction is left out of this copy.", terms: ["virtue-ethics", "universals", "eudaimonia"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato-ethics-politics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Plato's Ethics and Politics in The Republic” (Eric Brown)" },
  "classic-aristotle-ethics": { about: "Aristotle's inquiry into the highest good for human beings, which he calls happiness (eudaimonia), and into the virtues of character and intellect that living well takes. It is the later of his two ethical treatises; the Eudemian Ethics covers much the same ground.", built: "Ten books, each in short numbered chapters. In Peters's headings: the end (I), moral virtue (II), the will and the moral virtues (III–IV), justice (V), the intellectual virtues (VI), characters other than virtue and vice, and pleasure (VII), friendship (VIII–IX), pleasure and the conclusion (X).", terms: ["eudaimonia", "virtue-ethics", "teleology"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-ethics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle's Ethics” (Richard Kraut)" },
  "classic-descartes-meditations": { about: "Two works in one book. The Discourse on the Method (1637), written in French and published with three scientific essays, the Dioptrics, Meteorology and Geometry, tells how Descartes came to his method. The Meditations (1641), in Latin, put it to work: doubting all that can be doubted to find what is certain, then arguing from there that God exists and that mind and body are distinct.", built: "The Discourse in six parts; then the Meditations' dedication, preface and synopsis before the six Meditations themselves.", terms: ["rationalism", "skepticism", "dualism", "ontological-argument"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “René Descartes” (Gary Hatfield)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes-epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Descartes' Epistemology” (Lex Newman)" }] },
  "classic-hobbes-leviathan": { about: "Hobbes's case for an absolute sovereign, published in 1651. Starting from human nature, he argues that without a common power life would be a war of every man against every man, and that reason leads people to covenant to obey one.", built: "Four parts: Of Man, Of Commonwealth, Of a Christian Commonwealth, and Of the Kingdom of Darkness. The argument for the sovereign runs through Parts I and II; this edition modernizes the spelling.", terms: ["state-of-nature", "social-contract", "sovereignty", "materialism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hobbes's Moral and Political Philosophy” (Susanne Sreedhar and Sharon A. Lloyd)" },
  "classic-hume-enquiry": { about: "Hume's recasting of the first book of his Treatise of Human Nature (1739–40), whose poor reception he blamed on its manner more than its matter. It traces all our ideas to impressions, asks what justifies reasoning from cause to effect, and finds the answer in custom, not reason; later sections turn the results on free will, miracles and scepticism itself.", built: "Twelve sections, each short enough to read at a sitting.", terms: ["empiricism", "skepticism", "induction-problem"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “David Hume”" },
  "classic-rousseau-social-contract": { about: "Rousseau's account of the one kind of political association that could make obedience legitimate, published in 1762 and condemned at once in Geneva, and banned by the authorities in Paris. Each person gives himself wholly to the community, and the people, as sovereign, is guided by the general will.", built: "G. D. H. Cole's introduction, then four books: the social compact (I), sovereignty and law (II), government and its forms (III), and the workings of a state, through to civil religion (IV).", terms: ["social-contract", "general-will", "sovereignty", "state-of-nature"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rousseau/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jean Jacques Rousseau” (Christopher Bertram)", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/rousseau/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Jean-Jacques Rousseau” (James J. Delaney)" }] },
  "classic-kant-groundwork": { about: "Kant's search for the supreme principle of morality, published in 1785, before the Critique of Practical Reason (1788) and The Metaphysics of Morals (1797). It moves from ordinary moral thinking to the categorical imperative, and its last section rests that law on the freedom, or autonomy, of a rational will. Abbott's translation calls it Fundamental Principles; today it is usually known as the Groundwork.", built: "A preface and three sections, each a transition: from common moral knowledge to philosophy, from popular moral philosophy to a metaphysic of morals, and from that to a critique of practical reason.", terms: ["deontology", "categorical-imperative", "autonomy", "a-priori"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-moral/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Kant's Moral Philosophy” (Adam Cureton)", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Immanuel Kant” (Michael Rohlf)" }] },
  "classic-wollstonecraft-vindication": { about: "Published in 1792, out of the debate over education in revolutionary France, and opening with a letter to Talleyrand, whose plan would have confined girls to a domestic education. Wollstonecraft argues that women are as capable of reason and virtue as men, and that an education designed to make them pleasing has made them weak.", built: "The letter to Talleyrand, an introduction and thirteen chapters; Chapter V answers Rousseau and other writers on women's education.", terms: ["essentialism", "autonomy", "social-construction"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wollstonecraft/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Mary Wollstonecraft” (Sylvana Tomaselli)" },
  "classic-marx-engels-manifesto": { about: "Written by Marx and Engels as the platform of the Communist League; drawn up in January 1848, it went to a London printer a few weeks before the French revolution of that February. It argues that history is the history of class struggles, that the bourgeoisie has remade the world while creating the class that will overthrow it, and sets out what communists stand for. Samuel Moore's translation (1888) was approved by Engels, who added notes.", built: "Engels's preface to this edition and the short preamble, then four sections.", terms: ["historical-materialism", "class-consciousness", "ideology"], src: "https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/karl-marx_friedrich-engels/the-communist-manifesto/samuel-moore/text/single-page", srcName: "Friedrich Engels, preface to the English edition of 1888 (Standard Ebooks)" },
  "classic-mill-liberty": { about: "Mill's defense, published in 1859, of the individual's freedom of thought, speech and action against both governments and the pressure of majority opinion. Its harm principle allows power over anyone against their will only to prevent harm to others, and Mill rests it on utility, not on abstract right. He dedicated it to the memory of Harriet Taylor, his wife, calling her in part its author.", built: "W. L. Courtney's introduction, then five chapters: the principle (I), liberty of thought and discussion (II), individuality (III), the limits of society's authority (IV) and applications (V).", terms: ["harm-principle", "consequentialism"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill-moral-political/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Mill's Moral and Political Philosophy” (David Brink)" },
  "classic-nietzsche-genealogy": { about: "Published in 1887, the year after Beyond Good and Evil, to take further doctrines that book had only sketched. Its three essays trace how “good and evil” came to replace “good and bad” through the resentment of the weak; how guilt, bad conscience and punishment arose; and what ascetic ideals mean.", built: "An editor's note and Nietzsche's preface, then three essays.", terms: ["genealogy", "ressentiment", "will-to-power"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nietzsche/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Friedrich Nietzsche” (R. Lanier Anderson)" },
  "classic-cooper-voice": { about: "Essays and speeches published in 1892 by an educator; Beverly Guy-Sheftall calls it the first book-length Black feminist text. Cooper argues that raising up Black women is the measure and the means of the whole race's progress, and that their voice had been missing from the debate on race.", built: "Her preface, Our Raison d'Être, then two parts of four essays each: Soprano Obligato, on women, and Tutti ad Libitum, on race, American letters and belief.", terms: ["intersectionality", "standpoint"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anna-julia-cooper/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Anna Julia Cooper” (Kathryn Sophia Belle)" },
  "classic-du-bois-souls": { about: "Essays published in 1903, some printed before, in other form, in The Atlantic Monthly and other journals, on the lives of Black Americans after emancipation. Du Bois names the problem of the twentieth century as the problem of the color line, and describes double-consciousness, the sense of always looking at oneself through the eyes of others.", built: "The Forethought, fourteen chapters and The Afterthought. Each chapter opens with lines of verse and a phrase of music from a spiritual, the sorrow songs of the last chapter.", terms: ["double-consciousness", "racial-formation"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dubois/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “W.E.B. Du Bois” (Robert Gooding-Williams)" },
  "classic-russell-problems": { about: "A short introduction published in 1912, which Russell called his “shilling shocker.” As his preface says, it keeps to the problems on which he thought something positive and constructive could be said, chiefly problems of knowledge, and it closes with a chapter on the value of philosophy.", built: "Fifteen short chapters, from appearance and reality to the value of philosophy, then Russell's note on further reading.", terms: ["empiricism", "rationalism", "a-priori", "universals", "induction-problem"], src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Bertrand Russell” (Andrew David Irvine)", srcs: [{ src: "https://bracers.mcmaster.ca/17208", srcName: "Bertrand Russell Archives, McMaster University: Russell to Ottoline Morrell, 23 June 1911" }] },
};
